            <ArticleTitle>The natural history of isolated left ventricular diastolic dysfunction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>627-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the natural history of isolated left ventricular diastolic dysfunction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Follow-up (average duration, 68 months) was obtained in 51 patients with isolated left ventricular diastolic dysfunction at cardiac catheterization, characterized by (1) an elevated left ventricular end-diastolic pressure; (2) normal left ventricular end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes; (3) normal left ventricular ejection fraction; (4) no coronary artery disease; and (5) no valvular disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During follow-up, seven patients died, but only one died of cardiac causes. Of the 44 living subjects, 20 (45%) noted new-onset symptoms of congestive heart failure, with 11 (25%) of these requiring hospitalization, and 12 (27%) required hospitalization for recurrent chest pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Isolated left ventricular diastolic dysfunction is associated with a low cardiac mortality; at the same time, however, it is associated with substantial morbidity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
